Below is a ready‑to‑paste HTML snippet that you can drop into any web page.  
It embeds the new map (centered on the library), adds a **“State Library”** marker, and overlays three circles at the coordinates you specified.

```html
<!-- 1️⃣  Google Maps embed (roadmap, 600 × 400, zoom 15) -->
<iframe
    width="600"
    height="400"
    style="border:0"
    loading="lazy"
    allowfullscreen
    src="https://www.google.com/maps/embed/v1/view?key=YOUR_API_KEY
         &center=34.052235,-118.243683
         &zoom=15
         &maptype=roadmap">
</iframe>

<!-- 2️⃣  Add the State Library marker (via the Maps JavaScript API) -->
<script src="https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/js?key=YOUR_API_KEY"></script>
<script>
  // Initialise the map (same centre/zoom as the iframe above)
  function initMap() {
    const map = new google.maps.Map(document.getElementById('stateLibMap'), {
      center: { lat: 34.052235, lng: -118.243683 },
      zoom: 15,
      mapTypeId: 'roadmap'
    });

    // ---- Marker -------------------------------------------------
    new google.maps.Marker({
      position: { lat: 34.052235, lng: -118.243683 },
      map,
      title: 'State Library'
    });

    // ---- Circular overlays ----------------------------------------
    const circleOpts = {
      map,
      radius: 100,               // metres – adjust as needed
      fillColor: '#FF0000',
      fillOpacity: 0.30,
      strokeColor: '#FF0000',
      strokeWeight: 2
    };

    // 1st circle
    new google.maps.Circle({
      ...circleOpts,
      center: { lat: 34.052235, lng: -118.243683 }
    });

    // 2nd circle
    new google.maps.Circle({
      ...circleOpts,
      center: { lat: 34.053690, lng: -118.2423732 }
    });

    // 3rd circle
    new google.maps.Circle({
      ...circleOpts,
      center: { lat: 34.053810, lng: -118.242751 }
    });
  }

  // Load the map once the page is ready
  window.addEventListener('load', initMap);
</script>

<!-- 3️⃣  Container for the JavaScript‑based map (the circles & marker) -->
<div id="stateLibMap" style="width:600px;height:400px;margin-top:10px;"></div>
```

### How to use it

1. **Replace `YOUR_API_KEY`** with a valid Google Maps JavaScript API key (you can obtain one from the Google Cloud Console).  
2. The `<iframe>` gives a quick static view of the map; the JavaScript section below it creates a fully interactive map where the **State Library** marker and the three circles are drawn.  
3. Adjust the `radius` value in `circleOpts` if you need a larger or smaller circle.  

That’s it—once the snippet is placed on your page and the API key is set, visitors will see the library location marked and the three circular zones you requested.

