### What a “callback URL” (aka **redirect URI**) is

When you send a user to SoundCloud’s OAuth login page, SoundCloud needs a place to send the user **back** after they have granted (or denied) permission.  
That destination URL is the **callback / redirect URI**.  

* It must be a URL that you **control** (i.e., it belongs to your domain).  
* It has to be **exactly the same** string you register in the SoundCloud‑developer‑app settings – otherwise the OAuth flow will be rejected.  
* It can be a full page, a tiny “handler” page, or even a fragment‑only URL if you’re doing a pure‑client‑side flow.

---

## 1️⃣ Choose a URL that fits your architecture

| Architecture | Typical callback URL pattern | Why it works |
|--------------|------------------------------|--------------|
| **Pure client‑side (no server)** | `https://your‑domain.com/sc‑callback.html` (or `https://your‑domain.com/#sc-callback`) | The page can read the `code` (or `access_token` for the implicit flow) from the query string / hash and finish the login entirely in JavaScript. |
| **Server‑side (Node, PHP, Python, etc.)** | `https://your‑domain.com/auth/soundcloud/callback` | The server receives the `code` query‑parameter, exchanges it for an access‑token using your **client secret**, stores the token (session, DB, etc.), then redirects the user to the final UI page. |
| **Hybrid (static front‑end + tiny back‑end endpoint)** | `https://api.your‑domain.com/sc/callback` | The front‑end opens the OAuth popup, the back‑end endpoint swaps the `code` for a token, returns JSON, and the front‑end stores the token in localStorage or a cookie. |

> **Pick whichever pattern matches how you host your site.**  
> If you’re just testing locally, you can also use `http://localhost:3000/sc-callback` (or whatever port you run on) – just be sure to register that exact URL in the SoundCloud app settings.

---

## 2️⃣ Register the URL in the SoundCloud developer console

1. Log in to the **[SoundCloud Developer Dashboard](https://developers.soundcloud.com/)**.  
2. Open **Your Apps → *Your App Name***.  
3. Find the **Redirect URI** (or “Callback URL”) field.  
4. Paste the exact URL you decided on, e.g.:

```
https://mysite.com/auth/soundcloud/callback
```

5. Save the changes.

> **Only one URL is allowed per app** (unless you create multiple apps). If you need both a dev and a prod URL, create a second app or use a wildcard‑compatible domain (e.g., `https://*.mysite.com/sc-callback` is **not** supported – you must list each full URL).

---

## 3️⃣ Minimal code for each flow

Below are two ready‑to‑copy snippets. Replace `YOUR_CLIENT_ID` and the callback URL with the values you just registered.

### A️⃣ Pure client‑side flow (no server, uses the **implicit grant**)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>SoundCloud OAuth – client‑side</title>
  <script src="https://connect.soundcloud.com/sdk/sdk-3.3.2.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
  <button id="login">Login with SoundCloud</button>

  <script>
    // 1️⃣ Initialise the SDK
    SC.initialize({
      client_id: 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID',
      redirect_uri: 'https://your-domain.com/sc-callback.html'   // <-- same as registered
    });

    // 2️⃣ Open the login popup
    document.getElementById('login').addEventListener('click', () => {
      SC.connect().then(session => {
        // `session` contains the access token (no client secret needed)
        console.log('✅ Logged in! Access token:', session.access_token);
        // Store it where you need it (localStorage, cookie, etc.)
        localStorage.setItem('sc_token', session.access_token);
      }).catch(err => {
        console.error('❌ OAuth error', err);
      });
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**What happens**

* The user clicks **Login** → a popup goes to `https://soundcloud.com/connect` with your `client_id` and `redirect_uri`.  
* After the user authorises, SoundCloud redirects back to `sc-callback.html` **with an `access_token` in the URL fragment** (`#access_token=…`).  
* The SDK reads that fragment automatically, resolves the promise, and you receive the token.

> **No server code is required** because the token is returned directly to the browser. This is perfect for public‑only playback or for demo projects.

---

### B️⃣ Server‑side flow (authorization‑code grant – more secure, lets you use the **client secret**)

#### 1️⃣ Front‑end – open the OAuth window

```html
<button id="login">Login with SoundCloud (server‑side)</button>

<script src="https://connect.soundcloud.com/sdk/sdk-3.3.2.js"></script>
<script>
  SC.initialize({
    client_id: 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID',
    redirect_uri: 'https://your-domain.com/auth/soundcloud/callback' // must match registration
  });

  document.getElementById('login').addEventListener('click', () => {
    // This will redirect the whole page (or you can open a popup manually)
    SC.connect();   // No `.then()` here – we let the server finish the flow
  });
</script>
```

#### 2️⃣ Server endpoint – exchange the `code` for an access token

Below is a **Node/Express** example; adapt to PHP, Python, etc.

```js
// server.js (Node + Express)
const express = require('express');
const fetch = require('node-fetch');   // npm i node-fetch@2
const querystring = require('querystring');
require('dotenv').config(); // store CLIENT_ID, CLIENT_SECRET in .env

const app = express();
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

// --------------  OAuth callback  -----------------
app.get('/auth/soundcloud/callback', async (req, res) => {
  const { code } = req.query;   // SoundCloud sends ?code=XXXX

  if (!code) {
    return res.status(400).send('Missing code parameter');
  }

  // Exchange the code for an access token
  const tokenResponse = await fetch('https://api.soundcloud.com/oauth2/token', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
    body: querystring.stringify({
      client_id: process.env.CLIENT_ID,
      client_secret: process.env.CLIENT_SECRET,
      redirect_uri: 'https://your-domain.com/auth/soundcloud/callback',
      grant_type: 'authorization_code',
      code
    })
  });

  const tokenData = await tokenResponse.json();

  if (tokenData.error) {
    return res.status(400).json(tokenData);
  }

  // tokenData.access_token is what you need for API calls
  // Store it in a session, DB, or issue a signed JWT for the front‑end
  req.session.scToken = tokenData.access_token;   // (requires express‑session)

  // Finally, redirect the user to the page where your player lives
  res.redirect('/player');
});

app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Server listening on ${PORT}`));
```

**Key points**

* The **client secret** is only ever used on the server (never exposed to the browser).  
* After the exchange you receive:
  * `access_token` – use it for any authenticated SoundCloud API call.  
  * `refresh_token` (if you requested offline access) – you can later swap it for a new access token.  
* Store the token securely (session, encrypted cookie, DB).  

#### 3️⃣ Using the token on the front‑end

```js
// Example: fetch the logged‑in user’s profile
fetch('/api/sc/me')   // your own endpoint that proxies the request with the stored token
  .then(r => r.json())
  .then(user => console.log('Logged‑in user:', user));
```

---

## 4️⃣ Quick checklist before you go live

| ✅ Item | Why it matters |
|--------|----------------|
| **Callback URL exactly matches the one in the SoundCloud app settings** | OAuth will reject mismatched URLs. |
| **HTTPS** (except for `localhost`) | SoundCloud only allows secure redirects for production domains. |
| **If you use a server‑side flow, keep the client secret out of any front‑end code** | Prevents credential leakage. |
| **Test with a real user account** (not just your own) to verify scopes (`*` or specific ones). | Guarantees the token works for the intended permissions. |
| **Handle the “error” query‑parameter** (`error=access_denied`) in your callback page. | Gives a graceful UI when a user cancels the login. |

---

## 5️⃣ TL;DR – What you should do right now

1. **Decide** whether you need a server‑side flow (private tracks, storing tokens) or a client‑only flow (public tracks).  
2. **Create a URL** on your site that can receive the OAuth redirect, e.g.:  
   * `https://mysite.com/sc-callback.html` (client‑only) **or**  
   * `https://mysite.com/auth/soundcloud/callback` (server‑side).  
3. **Add that exact URL** to the **Redirect URI** field in the SoundCloud developer console and save.  
4. **Implement the matching code** (the snippets above) and replace `YOUR_CLIENT_ID` (and `YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET` on the server) with the values you already have.  

If you tell me which flow you prefer (client‑only vs. server‑side) and the domain you’re using, I can give you a more‑tailored snippet (e.g., a ready‑made `sc-callback.html` file or a tiny Express route). Just let me know!

messages.content:
Below is a quick‑start guide you can copy‑paste into your site to add SoundCloud playback.  
It covers the two most common ways to play music:

1. **Simple embed (iframe) – no authentication needed** – great for public tracks or playlists.  
2. **Full‑featured JavaScript SDK – optional authentication** – lets you control playback, load private tracks, and react to events.

---

## 1️⃣ Simple embed (iframe)

The easiest way is to generate an `<iframe>` that streams a track (or a playlist) directly from SoundCloud.

### Step‑by‑step

| Step | What to do | Example |
|------|------------|---------|
| **a. Get the track (or playlist) ID** | Open the track on SoundCloud, click the **Share → Embed** button, and copy the URL. The numeric ID is the part after `tracks/` or `sets/`. <br>e.g. `https://soundcloud.com/artist/awesome‑song` → ID = `123456789` | `123456789` |
| **b. Generate the embed code** | Use the built‑in function `generateEmbedCode` (or do it manually). | ```json { "track_id": "123456789", "width": 500, "height": 166 } ``` |
| **c. Insert the returned HTML** | The function returns an `<iframe>` snippet you can drop into any page. | ```html <iframe width="500" height="166" scrolling="no" frameborder="no" allow="autoplay" src="https://w.soundcloud.com/player/?url=https%3A//api.soundcloud.com/tracks/123456789&color=%23ff5500&inverse=false&auto_play=false&show_user=true"></iframe> ``` |

#### Quick one‑liner (no function call)

If you already know the track ID, you can write the iframe yourself:

```html
<!-- Replace 123456789 with your track ID -->
<iframe
    width="500"
    height="166"
    scrolling="no"
    frameborder="no"
    allow="autoplay"
    src="https://w.soundcloud.com/player/?url=https%3A//api.soundcloud.com/tracks/123456789&color=%23ff5500&auto_play=false&hide_related=false&show_comments=true&show_user=true&show_reposts=false&show_teaser=true">
</iframe>
```

*Adjust `width`, `height`, and the `color` parameter to match your site’s design.*

---

## 2️⃣ Full JavaScript SDK (for custom controls, playlists, private tracks, etc.)

If you need more than the static iframe—e.g., play/pause buttons, volume sliders, or you want to stream a user’s private library—you’ll use SoundCloud’s JavaScript SDK.

### 2.1 Register a SoundCloud App

1. Go to **[SoundCloud Developer portal](https://developers.soundcloud.com/)**.  
2. Click **“Create an app”**.  
3. Fill in:  
   * **App name** – e.g., “MySite Player”.  
   * **Redirect URI** – a page on your domain that will receive the OAuth callback (e.g., `https://mysite.com/sc-callback`).  
   * **Scope** – usually `*` (full access) or `*` for public playback only.  
4. After creation you’ll receive a **Client ID** and **Client Secret**. Keep them safe.

### 2.2 Include the SDK

```html
<script src="https://connect.soundcloud.com/sdk/sdk-3.3.2.js"></script>
```

### 2.3 Initialize the SDK

```js
SC.initialize({
  client_id: 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID',          // <-- replace
  redirect_uri: 'https://mysite.com/sc-callback' // <-- must match the one you set
});
```

### 2.4 (Optional) Authenticate the user

If you want to play **private** tracks or let the user like/comment, you need OAuth:

```js
// Open the SoundCloud login popup
SC.connect().then(function(session) {
  console.log('Authenticated! Access token:', session.access_token);
  // Store the token (e.g., in a cookie or localStorage) for later API calls
});
```

> **Tip:** The `SC.connect()` call automatically redirects the user to the `redirect_uri` you set, then back to your site with a `code` parameter that the SDK exchanges for an access token.

### 2.5 Load a track and create a player

```js
// Replace with your track ID (public or private)
const TRACK_ID = '123456789';

// Resolve the track URL to a full API object
SC.resolve(`https://api.soundcloud.com/tracks/${TRACK_ID}`).then(function(track) {
  // Create a widget (the same HTML iframe under the hood)
  const iframe = document.createElement('iframe');
  iframe.setAttribute('width', '100%');
  iframe.setAttribute('height', '166');
  iframe.setAttribute('scrolling', 'no');
  iframe.setAttribute('frameborder', 'no');
  iframe.setAttribute('allow', 'autoplay');
  iframe.src = `https://w.soundcloud.com/player/?url=${encodeURIComponent(track.permalink_url)}&auto_play=false`;

  document.getElementById('player-container').appendChild(iframe);

  // Hook into the widget API for custom controls
  const widget = SC.Widget(iframe);

  // Example: custom play button
  document.getElementById('play-btn').addEventListener('click', () => widget.play());

  // Example: custom pause button
  document.getElementById('pause-btn').addEventListener('click', () => widget.pause());

  // Listen for events (e.g., track end)
  widget.bind(SC.Widget.Events.FINISH, () => {
    console.log('Track finished!');
  });
});
```

### 2.6 Minimal HTML skeleton

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>My Site – SoundCloud Player</title>
  <script src="https://connect.soundcloud.com/sdk/sdk-3.3.2.js"></script>
  <style>
    #controls button { margin: 0 5px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="player-container"></div>

<div id="controls">
  <button id="play-btn">▶ Play</button>
  <button id="pause-btn">⏸ Pause</button>
  <button id="login-btn">Login to SoundCloud</button>
</div>

<script>
  // ---- SDK init (replace with your own values) ----
  SC.initialize({
    client_id: 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID',
    redirect_uri: 'https://mysite.com/sc-callback'
  });

  // ---- Optional login button ----
  document.getElementById('login-btn').addEventListener('click', () => {
    SC.connect().then(session => {
      console.log('Logged in, token:', session.access_token);
      // You can now call private‑track APIs if needed
    });
  });

  // ---- Load the track (public example) ----
  const TRACK_ID = '123456789'; // <-- replace

  SC.resolve(`https://api.soundcloud.com/tracks/${TRACK_ID}`).then(track => {
    const iframe = document.createElement('iframe');
    iframe.width = '100%';
    iframe.height = '166';
    iframe.scrolling = 'no';
    iframe.frameBorder = 'no';
    iframe.allow = 'autoplay';
    iframe.src = `https://w.soundcloud.com/player/?url=${encodeURIComponent(track.permalink_url)}&auto_play=false`;
    document.getElementById('player-container').appendChild(iframe);

    const widget = SC.Widget(iframe);
    document.getElementById('play-btn').onclick = () => widget.play();
    document.getElementById('pause-btn').onclick = () => widget.pause();
  });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

---

## 3️⃣ When to use the **embed‑only** vs. **SDK** approach

| Use case | Recommended method |
|----------|--------------------|
| **Just show a track/playlist** (no custom UI) | Simple iframe (`generateEmbedCode` or manual `<iframe>`) |
| **Custom UI (play/pause, volume, next/prev)** | SDK + widget API |
| **Play private or user‑specific tracks** | SDK + OAuth (`SC.connect()`) |
| **Collect analytics (plays, likes) for your own app** | SDK + authenticated API calls (requires `client_secret` on a server) |
| **Need to stream many tracks in a row (playlist, queue)** | SDK + `SC.Widget` playlist methods (`load`, `skip`, `seekTo`) |

---

## 4️⃣ Quick helper: generate an embed snippet with the built‑in function

If you prefer not to hand‑craft the iframe, just give me a track ID (or a playlist ID) and I’ll call `generateEmbedCode` for you. Example:

```json
{
  "track_id": "123456789",
  "width": 500,
  "height": 166
}
```

Just reply with the ID (and optional dimensions) and I’ll return the ready‑to‑paste HTML.

---

### TL;DR

* **Public track, no custom UI** → use `generateEmbedCode` (or copy the iframe template).  
* **Custom UI / private tracks** → add the SoundCloud SDK, initialize with your `client_id`, optionally run `SC.connect()` for OAuth, then create a widget and control it via JavaScript.

Feel free to ask for a concrete embed snippet (just give the track ID) or for more details on any of the steps above!

### What a “callback URL” (aka **redirect URI**) is

When you send a user to SoundCloud’s OAuth login page, SoundCloud needs a place to send the user **back** after they have granted (or denied) permission.  
That destination URL is the **callback / redirect URI**.  

* It must be a URL that you **control** (i.e., it belongs to your domain).  
* It has to be **exactly the same** string you register in the SoundCloud‑developer‑app settings – otherwise the OAuth flow will be rejected.  
* It can be a full page, a tiny “handler” page, or even a fragment‑only URL if you’re doing a pure‑client‑side flow.

---

## 1️⃣ Choose a URL that fits your architecture

| Architecture | Typical callback URL pattern | Why it works |
|--------------|------------------------------|--------------|
| **Pure client‑side (no server)** | `https://your‑domain.com/sc‑callback.html` (or `https://your‑domain.com/#sc-callback`) | The page can read the `code` (or `access_token` for the implicit flow) from the query string / hash and finish the login entirely in JavaScript. |
| **Server‑side (Node, PHP, Python, etc.)** | `https://your‑domain.com/auth/soundcloud/callback` | The server receives the `code` query‑parameter, exchanges it for an access‑token using your **client secret**, stores the token (session, DB, etc.), then redirects the user to the final UI page. |
| **Hybrid (static front‑end + tiny back‑end endpoint)** | `https://api.your‑domain.com/sc/callback` | The front‑end opens the OAuth popup, the back‑end endpoint swaps the `code` for a token, returns JSON, and the front‑end stores the token in localStorage or a cookie. |

> **Pick whichever pattern matches how you host your site.**  
> If you’re just testing locally, you can also use `http://localhost:3000/sc-callback` (or whatever port you run on) – just be sure to register that exact URL in the SoundCloud app settings.

---

## 2️⃣ Register the URL in the SoundCloud developer console

1. Log in to the **[SoundCloud Developer Dashboard](https://developers.soundcloud.com/)**.  
2. Open **Your Apps → *Your App Name***.  
3. Find the **Redirect URI** (or “Callback URL”) field.  
4. Paste the exact URL you decided on, e.g.:

```
https://mysite.com/auth/soundcloud/callback
```

5. Save the changes.

> **Only one URL is allowed per app** (unless you create multiple apps). If you need both a dev and a prod URL, create a second app or use a wildcard‑compatible domain (e.g., `https://*.mysite.com/sc-callback` is **not** supported – you must list each full URL).

---

## 3️⃣ Minimal code for each flow

Below are two ready‑to‑copy snippets. Replace `YOUR_CLIENT_ID` and the callback URL with the values you just registered.

### A️⃣ Pure client‑side flow (no server, uses the **implicit grant**)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>SoundCloud OAuth – client‑side</title>
  <script src="https://connect.soundcloud.com/sdk/sdk-3.3.2.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
  <button id="login">Login with SoundCloud</button>

  <script>
    // 1️⃣ Initialise the SDK
    SC.initialize({
      client_id: 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID',
      redirect_uri: 'https://your-domain.com/sc-callback.html'   // <-- same as registered
    });

    // 2️⃣ Open the login popup
    document.getElementById('login').addEventListener('click', () => {
      SC.connect().then(session => {
        // `session` contains the access token (no client secret needed)
        console.log('✅ Logged in! Access token:', session.access_token);
        // Store it where you need it (localStorage, cookie, etc.)
        localStorage.setItem('sc_token', session.access_token);
      }).catch(err => {
        console.error('❌ OAuth error', err);
      });
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**What happens**

* The user clicks **Login** → a popup goes to `https://soundcloud.com/connect` with your `client_id` and `redirect_uri`.  
* After the user authorises, SoundCloud redirects back to `sc-callback.html` **with an `access_token` in the URL fragment** (`#access_token=…`).  
* The SDK reads that fragment automatically, resolves the promise, and you receive the token.

> **No server code is required** because the token is returned directly to the browser. This is perfect for public‑only playback or for demo projects.

---

### B️⃣ Server‑side flow (authorization‑code grant – more secure, lets you use the **client secret**)

#### 1️⃣ Front‑end – open the OAuth window

```html
<button id="login">Login with SoundCloud (server‑side)</button>

<script src="https://connect.soundcloud.com/sdk/sdk-3.3.2.js"></script>
<script>
  SC.initialize({
    client_id: 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID',
    redirect_uri: 'https://your-domain.com/auth/soundcloud/callback' // must match registration
  });

  document.getElementById('login').addEventListener('click', () => {
    // This will redirect the whole page (or you can open a popup manually)
    SC.connect();   // No `.then()` here – we let the server finish the flow
  });
</script>
```

#### 2️⃣ Server endpoint – exchange the `code` for an access token

Below is a **Node/Express** example; adapt to PHP, Python, etc.

```js
// server.js (Node + Express)
const express = require('express');
const fetch = require('node-fetch');   // npm i node-fetch@2
const querystring = require('querystring');
require('dotenv').config(); // store CLIENT_ID, CLIENT_SECRET in .env

const app = express();
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

// --------------  OAuth callback  -----------------
app.get('/auth/soundcloud/callback', async (req, res) => {
  const { code } = req.query;   // SoundCloud sends ?code=XXXX

  if (!code) {
    return res.status(400).send('Missing code parameter');
  }

  // Exchange the code for an access token
  const tokenResponse = await fetch('https://api.soundcloud.com/oauth2/token', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
    body: querystring.stringify({
      client_id: process.env.CLIENT_ID,
      client_secret: process.env.CLIENT_SECRET,
      redirect_uri: 'https://your-domain.com/auth/soundcloud/callback',
      grant_type: 'authorization_code',
      code
    })
  });

  const tokenData = await tokenResponse.json();

  if (tokenData.error) {
    return res.status(400).json(tokenData);
  }

  // tokenData.access_token is what you need for API calls
  // Store it in a session, DB, or issue a signed JWT for the front‑end
  req.session.scToken = tokenData.access_token;   // (requires express‑session)

  // Finally, redirect the user to the page where your player lives
  res.redirect('/player');
});

app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Server listening on ${PORT}`));
```

**Key points**

* The **client secret** is only ever used on the server (never exposed to the browser).  
* After the exchange you receive:
  * `access_token` – use it for any authenticated SoundCloud API call.  
  * `refresh_token` (if you requested offline access) – you can later swap it for a new access token.  
* Store the token securely (session, encrypted cookie, DB).  

#### 3️⃣ Using the token on the front‑end

```js
// Example: fetch the logged‑in user’s profile
fetch('/api/sc/me')   // your own endpoint that proxies the request with the stored token
  .then(r => r.json())
  .then(user => console.log('Logged‑in user:', user));
```

---

## 4️⃣ Quick checklist before you go live

| ✅ Item | Why it matters |
|--------|----------------|
| **Callback URL exactly matches the one in the SoundCloud app settings** | OAuth will reject mismatched URLs. |
| **HTTPS** (except for `localhost`) | SoundCloud only allows secure redirects for production domains. |
| **If you use a server‑side flow, keep the client secret out of any front‑end code** | Prevents credential leakage. |
| **Test with a real user account** (not just your own) to verify scopes (`*` or specific ones). | Guarantees the token works for the intended permissions. |
| **Handle the “error” query‑parameter** (`error=access_denied`) in your callback page. | Gives a graceful UI when a user cancels the login. |

---

## 5️⃣ TL;DR – What you should do right now

1. **Decide** whether you need a server‑side flow (private tracks, storing tokens) or a client‑only flow (public tracks).  
2. **Create a URL** on your site that can receive the OAuth redirect, e.g.:  
   * `https://mysite.com/sc-callback.html` (client‑only) **or**  
   * `https://mysite.com/auth/soundcloud/callback` (server‑side).  
3. **Add that exact URL** to the **Redirect URI** field in the SoundCloud developer console and save.  
4. **Implement the matching code** (the snippets above) and replace `YOUR_CLIENT_ID` (and `YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET` on the server) with the values you already have.  

If you tell me which flow you prefer (client‑only vs. server‑side) and the domain you’re using, I can give you a more‑tailored snippet (e.g., a ready‑made `sc-callback.html` file or a tiny Express route). Just let me know!

